    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d239">

                <pb facs="0403" n="1867" sameAs="frus:frus1951v06p2/pg1867"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">690D.91/9–2851: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Gifford</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1951-09-28T19:00:00+01:00">September 28,
                            1951—7 p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1567. FonOff learns from UKUN Graham returning today and consideration being
                    given to-informal talk between Graham and US and UK dels before report
                    published. Although FonOff view not yet firm this question, preliminary thinking
                    is UK del shld not participate for fear of getting into bad graces of Nehru who,
                    it will be recalled, on publication Dixon report accused US–UK of having
                    influenced Dixon in preparation thereof.</p>
                <p>UK hesitation this respect, however, based on reasons not necessarily applicable
                    to US. Important points to bear in mind from UK viewpoint are (1) report shld
                    leave door open for further negotiation (if practicable by Graham himself) on
                    any unsettled issues and therefore (2) action under Art 36<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d239-f1"/> wld be preferable to action under Art 37.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gifford</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d239-f1" n="1" xml:id="d239-f1-content">
                <p>With respect to procedure under Article 36 of the U.N. Charter, see United
                    Nations, <hi rendition="#italic">Repertory of Practice</hi>, vol. <hi
                        rendition="#small-caps">ii</hi>, pp. 273 ff. and especially pp. 309 ff.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
